    "## 5.5 Model diagnostics & assumptions\n",
    "\n",
    "Honest accounting of where these models satisfy or violate classical assumptions —\n",
    "and why they remain fit for purpose.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Linear regression on stars:**\n",
    "- *Not truly continuous:* stars are ordinal and bounded [1, 5]. Residuals therefore cannot\n",
    "  be normally distributed (visible as diagonal striping in the residual plot), and raw\n",
    "  predictions can exceed the valid range. Consequences: p-values/CIs on coefficients should\n",
    "  not be taken literally; point predictions and R² remain meaningful for benchmarking.\n",
    "- *Heteroscedasticity:* checked below (Breusch-Pagan).\n",
    "- *Multicollinearity:* checked below (VIF) — VADER sentiment and NRC valence measure\n",
    "  overlapping constructs by design.\n",
    "- *Independence:* reviews cluster within businesses and users, mildly understating\n",
    "  standard errors. Acceptable for prediction; a mixed-effects model would be the\n",
    "  rigorous upgrade for inference.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Logistic regression:** distribution-free by comparison (no normality/homoscedasticity\n",
    "assumptions); class imbalance handled via balanced weights. Shares the multicollinearity\n",
    "and independence caveats above.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Bottom line:** these models are used for *prediction and feature benchmarking*, not\n",
    "coefficient-level causal inference. For a rating-scale-respecting alternative, an ordinal\n",
    "logistic or gradient-boosted tree model is the natural next step."
   ]
  },
