    "## 6. Conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Real features, real signal.** VADER sentiment correlates with stars at r = 0.71 across\n",
    "  the combined 30,000 reviews; the section-1 integrity checks guarantee no mock data reached\n",
    "  the models (iteration 2's failure mode is structurally blocked).\n",
    "- **Assumptions addressed, not ignored (section 5.5):** multicollinearity resolved\n",
    "  (max VIF 117 → 3.3 after dropping `vad_matched_words` as a predictor and\n",
    "  log-transforming word count); heteroscedasticity confirmed via Breusch-Pagan and\n",
    "  corrected with HC3 robust standard errors for inference; predictions clipped to the\n",
    "  valid [1, 5] range. Remaining known limitations: ordinal target and clustered\n",
    "  observations — models are used for prediction/benchmarking, not causal inference.\n",
    "- **RQ1:** the refined lexicon feature set reaches **R² = 0.61** predicting star ratings —\n",
    "  well above iteration 1's transformer-derived benchmark of 0.25, at a fraction of the\n",
    "  compute. (MSE is not directly comparable across iterations: these industry samples\n",
    "  contain far more 1-star reviews than the general Yelp sample.) Average error ≈ 1 star.\n",
    "- **RQ2:** positive/negative classification reaches **91% accuracy** (F1: 0.93 positive /\n",
    "  0.85 negative with balanced class weights). Sentiment and valence dominate; negation\n",
    "  count and negative-emotion proportions push toward negative predictions.\n",
    "- **RQ3:** both industries behave similarly (per-industry R² and top drivers in section 5),\n",
    "  with salon reviews skewing slightly more positive and expressive than restaurant reviews.\n",
    "- **Next:** these fitted relationships feed the dashboard's diagnostic and predictive views;\n",
    "  the optional `--transformers` features (`hf_*` columns) enable a direct\n",
    "  lexicon-vs-transformer comparison as a future section."
